\begin{claim} \label{clm: external_same}
    Let $S_v$ be a vertex gadget in $H$. The external vertices of $S_v$ belong to the same set in $\mathcal{P}$.
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
    Let $x$ and $y$ be the external vertices of $S_v$ with $x \in V_i$ and $y \in V_j$ for $i, j \in [s]$. Suppose toward a contradiction that $i \neq j$.
    If $V_i$ contains $p_i+1$ clique vertices of $S_v$, then the subgraph induced on $x$ and all clique vertices of $S_v$ in $V_i$ have minimum degree at least~$p_i+1$. Hence, at most $p_i$ clique vertices of $S_v$ are in $V_i$. 
    Likewise, at most $p_j$ clique vertices in $S_v$ are in $V_j$.
    Similarly, if there were $p_h+2$ clique vertices in $V_h$ for $h \in [s]$, $h\neq i, j$, then the subgraph induced on the clique vertices of $S_v$ in $V_k$ has minimum degree at least~$p_h+1$. Hence, at most $p_h+1$ clique vertices of $S_v$ are in $V_h$. Since
     \[\sum_{h=1, h \neq i,j}^s (p_h+1) + p_i + p_j = \sum_{i=1}^s p_i+s-2 < \sum_{i=1}^s p_i +s -1,\]
     there is a clique vertex of $S_v$ which is not contained in a set of $\mathcal{P}$, contradicting the fact that $\mathcal{P}$ is a partition.
    \end{proof}

\begin{claim} \label{clm: two_edges}
    Let $S_v$ and $S_u$ be matched gadgets in $H$. Let $x$ be an external vertex of $S_v$ with $x \in V_i$ and $y$ be an external vertex of $S_u$ with $y \in V_j$. Then, $j \neq i$. 
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose toward a contradiction that $i=j$. Note in both $S_v$ and $S_u$, there are exactly $p_i$ clique vertices in $V_i$. Thus, $G[V_i]$ has minimum degree $p_i+1$, so $G[V_i]$ is not $p_i$-degenerate and $\mathcal{P}$ is not a $p$-partition.
\end{proof}
    We color the vertices of $G$ with colors $i \in [s]$. For all $v \in V(G)$, there is a corresponding vertex gadget $S_v$ in $H$. 
    By Claim \ref{clm: external_same}, both external vertices of $S_v$ are in the same set $V_i$ for some $i \in [s]$. We assign $v$ the color $i$. We argue that this is a proper coloring. 
    Consider some edge $uv \in E(G)$. By construction, $S_v$ and $S_u$ are matched gadgets. Let $a \in V(S_v)$ be an external vertex with $a \in V_i$ and let $b \in V(S_u)$ be an external vertex with $b \in V_j$. By \cref{clm: two_edges}, $i \neq j$, so $u$ and $v$ are assigned different colors. Therefore, this is a proper coloring and thus, $G$ is $s$-colorable.

    \medskip
    \noindent
    We now suppose $G$ is $s$-colorable and show that $H$ is $p$-partitionable by assigning each vertex in~$H$ to some set $\mathcal{P}=\{V_1 \dots V_s\}$.
    Fix a proper coloring of $G$ with colors $i \in [s]$. For $v \in V(G)$ with color $i$, assign all external vertices of $S_v$ to the set $V_i$.
    Assign exactly $p_i$ clique vertices of $S_v$ to $V_i$ and $p_j+1$ clique vertices to $V_j$ for all $j \in [s]$ with $j \neq i$.

    To see that $\mathcal{P}$ is a $p$-partition, note first that $S_v[V_j]$ is a clique on $p_j+1$ vertices for all $j \in [s]$ with $j \neq i$, which is $p_j$-degenerate, and $S_v[V_i]$ is $p_i$-degenerate. 
    Now, consider two matched gadgets $S_u$ and $S_v$. 
    By construction, $uv \in E(G)$, so $u$ is assigned a different color from $v$. 
    Therefore, the external vertices of $S_u$ are in a set $V_i$ and the external vertices of~$S_v$ are in a set $V_j$ with $i \neq j$. 
    Hence, $S_v[V_i]$ is disconnected from $S_u[V_i]$ for all $i \in [s]$, so $(V_1, \dots, V_s)$ is a $p$-partition of $H$.

Let $H$ be a Rubin graph on vertices $v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_n$, where we have arranged the vertices as they appear along the cycle (possibly with one chord) defining~$H$. Let $L$ be any degree-list assignment.
    We consider cases. In what follows, we say $v_{i - 1} = v_n$ if $i = 1$ and $v_{i+1} = v_1$ if $i = n$.

    \medskip \noindent \textcolor{lipicsGray}{{\sffamily\bfseries Case 1.}} There exists a vertex $v_i$ with $|L(v_i)| > \deg(v_i)$.\\
    Without loss of generality, say $i = n$. Then we greedily color the vertices in order of increasing index. This is always possible, since $v_n$ has more available colors than neighbors. Moreover, there are at least two choices of color for $v_1$, yielding at least $2$ colorings.

    \medskip \noindent \textcolor{lipicsGray}{{\sffamily\bfseries Case 2.}} All lists are pairwise identical.\\
    If $H$ has a chord then we are in Case 1, so we may assume that $H$ is an induced even cycle. Then we observe that there are two ways to color an even cycle by two colors.

    \medskip \noindent \textcolor{lipicsGray}{{\sffamily\bfseries Case 3.}} There exists a vertex $v_i$ and a color $x$ such that $x \in L(v_i)$ and $x \notin L(v_{i - 1}) \cup L(v_{i+1})$.\\
    Without loss of generality, say $i = 1$. Fix color $x$ for $v_1$, and then color greedily in order of increasing index. Each vertex besides $v_n$ has at least one uncolored neighbor at the time that it is colored, and thus an available color. The vertex $v_n$ will have an available color since one of its neighbors received color $x \notin L(v_n)$. Finally, there are at least two choices of color for $v_2$ since $x \notin L(v_2)$.
    Notice that this argument works regardless of the endpoints of a chord, if one exists.

    \medskip \noindent \textcolor{lipicsGray}{{\sffamily\bfseries Case 4.}} There exists a vertex $v_i$ and two distinct colors $x, y$ such that $x, y \in L(v_i)$, $x \notin L(v_{i+1})$, and $y \notin L(v_{i-1})$.\\
    Without loss of generality, say $i = 1$. Produce one coloring by fixing color $y$ for $v_1$ and then greedily coloring in order of increasing index. Note that $v_n$ has an available color, since one of its neighbors received color $y \notin L(v_n)$. 
    Produce a second coloring by fixing color $x$ for $v_1$ and greedily coloring in order of decreasing index. Now, $v_2$ has an available color since one of its neighbors received color $x \notin L(v_2)$.
    Notice again that this argument works regardless of the endpoints of a chord, if one exists.

    \medskip \noindent \textcolor{lipicsGray}{{\sffamily\bfseries Case 5.}} There exists a vertex $v_i$ and two distinct colors $x, y$ such that $x, y \in L(v_i)$ and $x, y \notin L(v_{i - 1})$ or $x, y \notin L(v_{i+1})$.\\
    Without loss of generality, say $i = 1$, and suppose $x, y \notin L(v_n)$. Produce one coloring by fixing color $x$ for $v_1$ and then greedily coloring in order of increasing index. The vertex $v_n$ has an available color, since one of its neighbors received color $x \notin L(v_n)$. Produce a second coloring by fixing $y$ for $v_1$ and then coloring in order of increasing index. Once again $v_n$ has an available color, since one of its neighbors received color $y \notin L(v_n)$. The proof for the case where $x, y \notin L(v_{2})$ is identical, except that we greedily color in order of decreasing index. Finally, once again the argument does not depend upon the endpoints of a chord, if one exists.

    \medskip
    \noindent
    The cases above are not exhaustive, but they give us the tools we need to complete the proof. In what follows, we assume that none of these cases applies. 

    Assume first that $H$ has a chord, and say without loss of generality that $v_1$ is an endpoint of this chord. 
    Then $\deg(v_1) = 3$ and $\deg(v_2) = \deg(v_n) = 2$. Thus, if case 1 does not apply, then either case 3 or case 4 must apply.

    Then we may assume that $H$ is an induced even cycle with all lists of length $2$. Since Case 2 does not apply, not all lists are pairwise identical, and in particular we may assume without loss of generality that $L(v_1) \neq L(v_n)$. Because Case 5 does not apply, $L(v_1)$ and $L(v_n)$ have exactly one common color. Concretely, we may write $L(v_1) = \{a, b\}$ and $L(v_n) = \{b, c\}$. Because neither Case 3 nor 4 applies, we may assume that $L(v_2) = L(v_1) = \{a, b\}$.                                                       

    We now consider two greedy procedures producing (not necessarily distinct) $L$-colorings $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ of $H$. We set $\phi_1(v_1) = a$. We then complete $\phi_1$ by greedily coloring in order of increasing index. There will be an available color for $v_n$, since $a \notin L(v_n)$. Notice that $\phi_1(v_2) = b$. Similarly, we form $\phi_2$ by setting $\phi_2(v_n) = c$ and then greedily coloring in order of decreasing index.

    If $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ differ on any vertex then we found two colorings, so we may assume that
    \begin{align}
        \phi_1(v_i) = \phi_2(v_i) \text{ for all } i \in [n].\label{eq:rubin-coloring:identical-colorings}
    \end{align}

    Now suppose there exists some vertex $v_i$, for $i > 1$, such that $\phi_1(v_{i - 1}) \notin L(v_i)$. Then $v_i$ had two available colors during the greedy coloring procedure defining $\phi_1$, and we may form a second coloring $\phi_1'$ by changing the color of $v_i$ and then continuing the greedy procedure as before.
    Thus, we may assume that
    \begin{align}
        L(v_i) = \{\phi_1(v_{i - 1}), \phi_1(v_i)\} \text{ for all } i > 1.\label{eq:rubin-coloring:list1}
    \end{align}
    By a symmetric argument, we may assume that
    \begin{align}
        L(v_i) = \{\phi_2(v_{i}), \phi_2(v_{i+1})\} \text{ for all } i < n.\label{eq:rubin-coloring:list2}
    \end{align}

    We now complete the proof by showing that equations (\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:identical-colorings}), (\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:list1}), and (\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:list2}) cannot all be true. Assume otherwise. Then by combining equations (\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:identical-colorings}), (\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:list1}), and (\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:list2}) we obtain the following.
    \begin{align*}
        L(v_i) = \{\phi_1(v_{i - 1}), \phi_1(v_i), \phi_1(v_{i+1})\} \text{ for all } 1 < i < n.
    \end{align*}
    Recalling that we have already assumed Case 1 does not apply, this implies that $\phi_1(v_{i-1}) = \phi_1(v_{i+1})$ for all $1 < i < n$.
    Since $\phi_1(v_1) = a$ and $\phi_1(v_2) = b$, it follows by induction that for all $i \in [n],$
    \begin{align*}
        \phi_1(v_i) = \begin{cases}
                        a \quad\text{if $i$ is odd}\\
                        b \quad\text{if $i$ is even}.\\
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

    Since $n$ is even, this implies that $\phi_1(v_n) = b$, and by equation~(\ref{eq:rubin-coloring:identical-colorings}) we have $\phi_2(v_n) = b$, but this contradicts the definition of $\phi_2$. This completes the proof.
